.TH test "" "Command"
.SH NAME
test \- evaluate conditional expression
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtest \fIexpression ...\fR
.br
\fB[ \fIexpression ...\fB ]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
test -- evaluate conditional expression
Usage:	test expression ...
Expression:
	-d file		'file' exists and is a directory
	-f file		'file' exists and is not a directory
	-n string	'string' is of nonzero length
	-r file		'file' exists and is readable
	-s file		'file' exists and has nonzero size
	-t [fd]		File descriptor fd (default 1) is a terminal
	-w file		'file' exists and is writable
	-z string	'string' is of zero length
	string		'string' is not zero-length
	s1 = s2		's1' and 's2' are equal
	s1 != s2	's1' and 's2' are not equal
	n1 -eq n2	Numbers 'n1' and 'n2' are equal
	n1 -ne n2	'n1' and 'n2' are unequal
	n1 -gt n2	'n1' is greater than 'n2'
	n1 -ge n2	'n1' is greater than or equal to 'n2'
	n1 -lt n2	'n1' is less than 'n2'
	n1 -le n2	'n1' is less than or equal to 'n2'
	! exp		Negate the truth value of expression 'exp'
	exp1 -a exp2	Expressions 'exp1' and 'exp2' are both true
	exp1 -o exp2	Expression 'exp1' or 'exp2' is true
	( exp )		Grouping
.HE
.B test
evaluates
.I expression
and sets its exit status to reflect the result: zero (true) if
.I expression
is true, nonzero (false) if it is false.
It is used from shell scripts, typically as the condition of an
.B if
or
.B while
statement, to test file attributes or to compare strings and
numbers.
.I expression
is built from the primitives below, combined with
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR ! ,
and parentheses.
.B \-a
binds more tightly than
.BR \-o .
.IP "\fB\-d \fIfile\fR"
True if
.I file
exists and is a directory.
.IP "\fB\-f \fIfile\fR"
True if
.I file
exists and is not a directory.
.IP "\fB\-n \fIstring\fR"
True if
.I string
is of nonzero length.
.IP "\fB\-r \fIfile\fR"
True if
.I file
exists and is readable.
.IP "\fB\-s \fIfile\fR"
True if
.I file
exists and has nonzero size.
.IP "\fB\-t \fR[\fIfd\fR]"
True if file descriptor
.I fd
(default 1, standard output) is associated with a terminal.
.IP "\fB\-w \fIfile\fR"
True if
.I file
exists and is writable.
.IP "\fB\-z \fIstring\fR"
True if
.I string
is of zero length.
.IP \fIstring\fR
True if
.I string
is not the null string; equivalent to \fB\-n \fIstring\fR.
.IP "\fIs1\fB = \fIs2\fR"
True if strings
.I s1
and
.I s2
are equal.
.IP "\fIs1\fB != \fIs2\fR"
True if strings
.I s1
and
.I s2
are not equal.
.IP "\fIn1 \fB\-eq \fIn2\fR"
True if numbers
.I n1
and
.I n2
are equal.
.IP "\fIn1 \fB\-ne \fIn2\fR"
True if
.I n1
and
.I n2
are unequal.
.IP "\fIn1 \fB\-gt \fIn2\fR"
True if
.I n1
is greater than
.IR n2 .
.IP "\fIn1 \fB\-ge \fIn2\fR"
True if
.I n1
is greater than or equal to
.IR n2 .
.IP "\fIn1 \fB\-lt \fIn2\fR"
True if
.I n1
is less than
.IR n2 .
.IP "\fIn1 \fB\-le \fIn2\fR"
True if
.I n1
is less than or equal to
.IR n2 .
.IP "\fB! \fIexp\fR"
Negate the truth value of
.IR exp .
.IP "\fIexp1 \fB\-a \fIexp2\fR"
True if both
.I exp1
and
.I exp2
are true.
.IP "\fIexp1 \fB\-o \fIexp2\fR"
True if either
.I exp1
or
.I exp2
is true.
.IP "\fB( \fIexp \fB)\fR"
Groups
.I exp
to override the default binding of
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-o ,
and
.BR ! .
.PP
When invoked under the name
.BR [ ,
.B test
requires that its last argument be a literal
.BR ] ,
allowing the alternative form
\fB[ \fIexpression \fB]\fR
in place of
\fBtest \fIexpression\fR.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR expr ,
.B sh
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B test
prints the message `Test expression syntax error' and returns a
nonzero status if
.I expression
is malformed.
